DOOM for OS/2
=============

A port of the released DOOM source to OS/2 2.x and Warp, built with Open
Watcom 1.9.  The engine is id's, unchanged apart from the handful of places
listed under "Changes to the engine" below; everything OS/2 about it lives in
the five platform modules.

Requires a real DOOM WAD, as every build of DOOM does.


Building
--------

    OS2\BUILD\MKOS2.CMD

This is a *cross build*.  It runs on a Windows machine with Open Watcom 1.9
installed -- Watcom's NT-hosted compiler emits OS/2 LX objects -- and produces
BUILD\DOOM.EXE for OS/2.  The result cannot be run on the machine that built
it.  Set %WATCOM% if Open Watcom is not at C:\WATCOM.

Object files stay in BUILD\, out of the source tree.

The build has been verified to compile and link clean.  It has *not* been run
on OS/2; see "What has not been tested" at the end.


Running
-------

Put DOOM.EXE next to a WAD, or point DOOMWADDIR at one:

    SET DOOMWADDIR=D:\GAMES\DOOM
    DOOM

Run it from an OS/2 window, not a full screen session -- see "Why it is a VIO
program" below.  The configuration is written to DEFAULT.CFG beside the WADs.

Command line options, beyond the ones every DOOM has:

    -2, -3          Open the window at two or three times 320x200.  The
                    window can be resized freely afterwards regardless; the
                    blitter scales to whatever size it is.
    -nodive         Skip DIVE and use the GPI fallback.  Useful for telling
                    the two apart when something looks wrong.
    -nograbmouse    Leave the mouse pointer alone.  Without this the pointer
                    is captured and hidden whenever the window is active.
    -nosound        Do not open the audio device at all.
    -mb <n>         Zone heap size in megabytes.  Default 6.  DOS DOOM had
                    this; the Linux build did not.


How it works
------------

**Video.**  Two paths, chosen at startup and reported on the console.

  DIVE (Direct Interface Video Extensions) is the fast one.  DOOM's 320x200
  8-bit frame buffer is handed to DIVE, which blits it to the visible part of
  the window with the display driver doing the scaling and colour conversion.
  This is as close to the hardware as a PM program is allowed to get.

  GpiDrawBits is the fallback, and works on a plain OS/2 2.0 with no
  multimedia installed.  It goes through the whole graphics engine every
  frame, so it is slower, but it is correct.

  DIVE.DLL is loaded by name at run time rather than imported, precisely so
  that a machine without it still runs the game.

  DOOM draws straight into the buffer the blitter reads -- I_InitGraphics
  points screens[0] at it -- so there is no copy between the renderer and the
  screen.  The X11 original did the same thing with the XImage's data.

**Sound.**  MMPM/2 DART, at 11025 Hz 16-bit stereo, which is what DOOM's
  mixer produces.

  The mixing itself is id's, untouched.  What changed is who calls it.  On
  Linux the game loop mixed a buffer per rendered frame and wrote it to
  /dev/dsp; that only worked because the write blocked when the driver's
  buffer filled, and that blocking is what held the frame rate down to
  whatever the sound card was draining at.  Under DART, MMPM/2 calls back on
  a thread of its own each time a buffer has played, and the mixing happens
  there -- at exactly the rate the hardware consumes it, with the game loop
  left alone.  I_UpdateSound and I_SubmitSound are consequently empty.

  That second thread is the reason every module is compiled -bm.  The channel
  table that the callback reads and I_StartSound writes is held under a mutex
  semaphore.

  There is still no music.  The Linux original had none either.

  MDM.DLL is loaded by name, like DIVE.  No sound card, no MMPM/2, or another
  program holding the device: DOOM says so and plays silently.

**Networking.**  IBM TCP/IP, over the same UDP packets and the same port
  (5029) as every other DOOM, so this can play against linuxxdoom.

  Three things differ from Berkeley sockets and all three bite: sock_init()
  has to be called before anything works, soclose() closes a socket where
  close() does not, and errno says nothing -- sock_errno() does.

  SO32DLL and TCP32DLL are loaded by name, and only when -net is actually
  used.  A single player game on a machine with no TCP/IP never notices.

**No hard dependencies.**  The upshot of all that name-loading is that
DOOM.EXE imports nothing but the base system:

    DOSCALLS  NLS  KBDCALLS  VIOCALLS  PMWIN  PMGPI

It loads and plays on a bare OS/2 2.0, and picks up DIVE, sound and
networking on any machine that has them.

  One trap worth recording, since it is invisible until you look: calling
  htons() or htonl() would have put an import on TCPIP32.DLL into the
  executable and undone the whole arrangement.  They are real functions in
  OS/2's library, not macros.  I_NET.C defines its own, which is what the
  Linux original did too -- "for some odd reason", as its comment has it.


Why it is a VIO program
-----------------------

DOOM.EXE is linked "sys os2v2" -- a text-mode program -- and turns *itself*
into a Presentation Manager program when it opens its window, by changing its
own process type in its process information block.  I_OS2_MorphToPM does it.

This is a documented OS/2 facility and it exists for exactly this case.  A
program linked as PM from the start has nowhere to put text, and DOOM has a
great deal to say before its window opens: the whole "W_Init: Init WADfiles"
banner, and more to the point the message when a WAD cannot be found.
Morphing keeps that text in the OS/2 window the game was launched from and
still gets a PM window a moment later.

It does mean starting DOOM from an OS/2 *window*, not a full screen session.
If that is a problem, change one word in MKOS2.CMD -- "sys os2v2_pm" instead
of "sys os2v2" -- and it builds as a pure PM application with no source
change at all.  Everything printed before the window opens is then lost,
which is why I_Error also puts its message in a message box.


Changes to the engine
---------------------

The engine sources here are id's, and the diff against linuxdoom-1.10 is
deliberately small.  In full:

  d_main.c    An __OS2__ branch alongside the NORMALUNIX one for the WAD
              search paths.  Same DOOMWADDIR behaviour; what changes is that
              there is no $HOME to insist on -- OS/2 has no per-user home
              directory that is guaranteed to exist, and refusing to start
              without one would stop the game on an ordinary Warp desktop.
              The configuration goes in DEFAULT.CFG beside the WADs.

              <direct.h> for mkdir, and the -cdrom call given the one
              argument OS/2's mkdir takes.

  w_wad.c     The unconditional "#define O_BINARY 0" is now guarded.  OS/2
              has a real text/binary distinction and <fcntl.h> already
              defines O_BINARY properly; flattening it to zero would stop
              every WAD read at the first 0x1a byte.

              strupr and filelength are no longer defined on OS/2 -- Watcom's
              run-time library has both, with different return types, and
              redefining them is a hard error.

  m_menu.c    O_BINARY on the savegame header read, for the same reason.

  m_bbox.h    <values.h> is a Unix header.  doomtype.h already defines MAXINT
              and MININT for every platform that is not Linux.

  r_data.c    alloca is declared in <malloc.h> on OS/2, not <alloca.h>.
              Without the prototype every stack buffer in that file arrives
              as a truncated pointer.

  doomdef.h   SNDSERV off.  There is no separate sound server process to
              fork and exec; the sound is integrated.

  i_main.c, i_system.c, i_video.c, i_sound.c, i_net.c
              Replaced entirely.  These are the port.

  os2doom.h   New: the PM handles and the two services -- morphing, and an
              error box -- that more than one platform module needs.

Everything else is byte-identical to the release.


What has not been tested
------------------------

The port compiles and links clean, and the executable has been verified to be
a proper LX binary for the 80386 under OS/2, flagged windowable-VIO, with the
import list above.  Nothing has been run: there is no OS/2 machine here.

The things most likely to want attention on first run, in the order I would
check them:

  1. The DIVE image orientation.  SETUP_BLITTER's fInvert is FALSE, which
     says the source is stored top-down -- which is how DOOM has always held
     its frame buffer.  If the picture comes out upside down on some display
     driver, that flag is the one to turn over.  It is commented in
     I_VIDEO.C.

  2. The keyboard.  Keys are read from PM's scan codes, because those are the
     only thing that reports shift, control and alt going up as well as down
     -- and DOOM uses all three.  The arrow keys and the grey navigation
     block send an 0xe0 prefix on the wire, and how much of that survives
     into WM_CHAR's scan code byte has varied between OS/2 keyboard drivers.
     I_TranslateKey falls back to the virtual key and then the character
     code, so it should cope either way, but this is the part most worth
     watching.

  3. DART buffer sizes.  MMPM/2 is free to hand back a different buffer size
     from the one asked for.  The callback fills whole mixes and reports how
     much it wrote, and refuses to start if the buffer is too small to hold
     even one -- but the size it actually gives is printed at startup, and is
     worth a glance.

  4. The socket entry point names in SO32DLL and TCP32DLL.  If one is wrong,
     -net fails at startup with a clear message and single player is
     unaffected.


Adding an icon
--------------

There is none.  The window gets the default program icon.

To add one, put a genuine OS/2 icon -- saved by OS/2's own ICONEDIT, or taken
from \OS2\BITMAP -- in a resource script, compile it with "wrc -bt=os2 -r",
and bind it into DOOM.EXE with a second wrc pass, then give the frame
FCF_ICON in I_InitGraphics.

Do not feed wrc a Windows .ICO for an OS/2 target.  It accepts it, exits 0,
and embeds it verbatim; PM then rejects it, WinLoadPointer fails, that fails
FCF_ICON, and that fails the whole WinCreateStdWindow with PM error 0x1034,
PMERR_INVALID_RESOURCE_FORMAT.


